  - [1mStore + a settings panel[0m — adds a settings UI section to [38;5;153mindex.html[39m with several controls. Largest scope; worth it
    only if you know you want a settings screen now.

  One constraint I'll hold regardless of your answer, since this is a login form: [1mno password is ever written to [0m
  [1mstorage.[0m A "remember me" style preference persists the username only. Persisting a credential to [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m exposes
  it to any script on the page, and I won't design that in without you explicitly asking.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What's in scope for this change? → Store + one preference (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I present the design.

  This repo has no tooling configured at all — no test runner, no linter, no build step, zero dependencies. Setting
  these up is cheapest now, before the code exists. The one that isn't optional in my view is [1munit tests[0m: a storage
  module is exactly the kind of thing where the edge cases (missing key, corrupted JSON, storage disabled in private
  browsing) are where the bugs live, and those are painful to trigger by hand in a browser.

  My recommendation is Node's built-in test runner ([38;5;153mnode:test[39m) — no dependency, keeps the repo at zero deps, and it
  works if I design the module to take its storage backend as a parameter, which also makes the [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m-unavailable
  case testable.

  Linting is cheap here too but genuinely optional at this size. End-to-end tests I'd skip: there's no server and one
  page.
